---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import { Aside } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import source from '../../../plugins/index.json?raw';
import { installCommand, sourceUrl, validate } from '../../lib/plugins/catalogue';

const { plugins } = validate(source);
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const repo = 'https://github.com/usecornercase/cornercase-terminal';
const platforms = (list?: string[]) => (list?.length ? list.join(', ') : 'macOS and Linux');
---
<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Plugin catalogue',
    description: 'Plugins reviewed for cornercase, each pinned to the commit that was read. Install one from Settings or the command line.',
  }}
>
  <p>
    Each plugin here was read by a cornercase maintainer at the commit it is pinned to, and installs exactly that commit.
    What a plugin is and how to write one is in the <a href={`${base}docs/guides/plugins/`}>plugins guide</a>. The list is
    also published as <a href={`${base}plugins/index.json`}><code>index.json</code></a>, which cornercase reads.
  </p>

  <h2 id="plugins">Plugins</h2>
  {plugins.length === 0 ? (
    <p data-empty>No plugins are listed yet. Yours could be the first: see <a href="#submit">submit a plugin</a>.</p>
  ) : (
    plugins.map((plugin) => (
      <section data-plugin={plugin.id}>
        <h3>{plugin.name} <small>{plugin.version}</small></h3>
        <p>{plugin.description}</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Source: <a href={sourceUrl(plugin)} rel="noopener noreferrer">{plugin.repo}</a>{plugin.ref ? ` at ${plugin.ref}` : ''}, commit <code>{plugin.commit.slice(0, 12)}</code></li>
          <li>Runs on: {platforms(plugin.platforms)}</li>
          <li>Asks for: {plugin.permissions?.length ? plugin.permissions.join(', ') : 'nothing'} (shown, not enforced)</li>
          {plugin.min_cornercase && <li>Needs cornercase {plugin.min_cornercase} or later</li>}
        </ul>
        <pre><code>{installCommand(plugin)}</code></pre>
      </section>
    ))
  )}

  <h2 id="install">Install one</h2>
  <p>
    In cornercase, open <strong>Settings › Plugins</strong> and click <strong>catalogue</strong> under Available. Only then
    does cornercase fetch this list; it never fetches it in the background. Click a plugin to see where it comes from
    and every command it will run, then <strong>install</strong>. From a shell, the command under each plugin installs
    the same commit and asks first.
  </p>
  <p>
    A plugin is installed turned off. Nothing in it runs until you turn it on in Settings or with
    <code>cornercase plugin enable &lt;id&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <Aside type="caution" title="A plugin runs as you">
    Review is not a sandbox. Once on, a plugin can do anything you can, including typing into every terminal.
    The permissions it lists are shown, not enforced. <code>cornercase plugin install owner/repo</code> also installs
    plugins that are not listed here, which nobody has reviewed.
  </Aside>

  <h2 id="submit">Submit a plugin</h2>
  <p>
    Open a pull request on <a href={repo}>cornercase-terminal</a> that adds an entry to
    <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/site/plugins/index.json`}><code>site/plugins/index.json</code></a>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`{
  "id": "acme.notes",
  "name": "Notes",
  "description": "A notes file per workspace",
  "repo": "acme/cornercase-notes",
  "ref": "v1.0.0",
  "commit": "<the full 40-character commit>",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "min_cornercase": "0.12.91",
  "platforms": ["macos", "linux"],
  "permissions": ["read", "send"],
  "sha256": { "aarch64-apple-darwin": "<sha256 of that download>" }
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, <code>repo</code> (<code>owner/repo</code> on GitHub),
    <code>commit</code> and <code>version</code> are required. The <code>id</code> must be the one in the plugin's
    <code>cornercase-plugin.toml</code> at that commit. When the manifest has a <code>[download]</code>, list the sha256 of
    each target here too: cornercase refuses a download that is not the one reviewed. <code>npm run check</code> in
    <code>site/</code> checks the format.
  </p>
  <p>A maintainer then reads, at that commit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the manifest: every action, hook, service and window process, the build steps and the downloads;</li>
    <li>the code those commands run, and what it does with the cornercase commands, your files and the network;</li>
    <li>that the declared permissions match what the code does;</li>
    <li>that a download is built from that commit, and its sha256.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A new version is a new pull request that moves the <code>commit</code>, reviewed the same way. A plugin found doing
    something it should not is removed from the list.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>
